## Issues

- [x] `src/.aide` outcomes.desired[0]: prior issue — spec enumerated five tools, server shipped six (aide_upgrade missing).
  Resolved: src/.aide was updated to enumerate upgrade as the sixth lifecycle phase.
  Traces to: outcomes.desired[0]
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [ ] `src/index.ts` lines 17–18, 148–196: `aide_info` and `aide_inspect` are registered and fully described in the handshake but are absent from `src/.aide` outcomes.desired[0]'s tool-surface enumeration. The spec states: "Each tool covers a distinct lifecycle phase with no overlap between them" and the undesired block explicitly warns against tools added because a responsibility "didn't quite fit" an existing tool. Neither aide_info nor aide_inspect is named in the spec, and no outcome text justifies why they cannot be absorbed or explains what distinct lifecycle phase each covers. The spec's tool-surface enumeration has drifted behind the implementation a second time.
  Traces to: outcomes.desired[0] (tool surface must cover full AIDE lifecycle with no overlap), outcomes.undesired[6] (tool added because responsibility didn't fit, when it could be absorbed — or in this case, when no spec rationale exists at all)
  Misalignment: spec-gap

## Retro

The same drift pattern recurred: a tool was added to the registered surface without updating `src/.aide`'s tool-surface outcome. The prior issue introduced a fix for aide_upgrade but did not introduce a process gate to prevent the same gap for future tools. Any commit that adds a new `case` to the `CallToolRequestSchema` handler in `src/index.ts` should trigger a review of `src/.aide` outcomes.desired[0] to confirm the enumeration still matches. The spec-writer should run `aide_discover` on the tools/ subtree after any tool addition and verify the parent outcome still covers what's shipped.
